.TH "vsearch-fastq" "5" "April 29, 2026" "version 2.31.0" "vsearch file formats"
.hy
.SH NAME
.PP
fastq \[em] a text-based format for representing nucleotide sequences
and their corresponding quality scores
.SH DESCRIPTION
.PP
In fastq files, each entry consists of four parts: a \f[I]sequence
header\f[R] starting with `\[at]', a \f[I]sequence\f[R], a \f[I]quality
header\f[R] starting with `+', and a \f[I]quality string\f[R]:
.IP
.nf
\f[C]
\[at]label [description]
ACGTURYSWKMDBHVN...
+
IIIIIIIIIIIIIIII...
\f[R]
.fi
.SS Sequence header
.PP
The \f[I]sequence header\f[R] is the line starting with `\[at]'.
The \f[I]label\f[R] is the string between `\[at]' and the first space,
tab, or newline, unless \f[V]--notrunclabels\f[R] is in effect, in which
case the entire line after `\[at]' is used as the label.
.PP
The header should contain printable ASCII characters (values 33\[en]126,
see \f[V]ascii(7)\f[R]).
The control characters 1\[en]8, 11\[en]12, 14\[en]31 and 127 cause a
fatal error; the tabulation (9) is accepted; a NUL byte (0) silently
truncates the label; carriage returns (13) and newlines (10) terminate
the line.
Non-ASCII characters (values 128\[en]255) trigger a warning.
This check covers the part of the header the command retains: the label
by default, or the whole header line for the filtering commands
(\f[V]--fastq_filter\f[R], \f[V]--fastx_filter\f[R]) and when
\f[V]--notrunclabels\f[R] is in effect.
.SS Sequence
.PP
The \f[I]sequence\f[R] is the line following the header line.
It is a string of IUPAC symbols (`ACGTURYSWKMDBHVN' and
`acgturyswkmdbhvn').
Carriage returns before the newline are accepted (Windows line endings);
any other character \[em] including tabulations, spaces, and any
non-IUPAC letter or non-ASCII byte \[em] causes a fatal error.
Unlike the fasta parser, nothing is silently stripped from fastq
sequences.
.SS Quality header
.PP
The \f[I]quality header\f[R] is the line starting with `+'.
The text after `+' must be empty or repeat the sequence header line
exactly; anything else causes a fatal error.
.SS Quality string
.PP
The \f[I]quality string\f[R] is a string of ASCII characters, one per
base, encoding the Phred quality score for the corresponding position.
The quality string starts after the quality header line and ends before
the next sequence header or the end of the file.
.PP
Quality scores are encoded as \f[V]Q = ASCII_value - offset\f[R], where
the offset is set with \f[V]--fastq_ascii\f[R] (default 33):
.IP \[bu] 2
\f[B]phred+33\f[R] (offset 33): Sanger and Illumina 1.8+ format.
Valid quality characters range from `!' (Q=0) to `\[ti]' (Q=93).
vsearch accepts scores from 0 to 41 by default (\f[V]--fastq_qmin\f[R]
and \f[V]--fastq_qmax\f[R]).
.IP \[bu] 2
\f[B]phred+64\f[R] (offset 64): Illumina 1.3+ and Illumina 1.5+ formats.
Valid quality characters range from `\[at]' (Q=0) to `\[ti]' (Q=62).
.PP
The older Solexa/Illumina 1.0 format shares the offset 64 but not the
score definition, and \f[B]vsearch does not support it\f[R].
A Solexa score is \f[V]Q = -10 log10(p / (1 - p))\f[R], not the Phred
\f[V]Q = -10 log10(p)\f[R], and in the Illumina 1.0 pipeline it ranges
from -5 to 40 (`;' to `h').
vsearch always applies the Phred formula, so a Solexa quality string is
read as if it were a Phred one: the error probability comes out 1.3
times too large at Q=5, 2 times at Q=0, and 4.2 times at Q=-5, where the
Phred formula returns 3.16 \[em] not a probability at all.
The two definitions converge as the score rises (3% apart at Q=15, 1% at
Q=20, 0.1% at Q=30), so only the low scores are affected, but those are
the ones a quality filter acts on.
Convert such files to phred+33 with a dedicated tool before feeding them
to vsearch.
.PP
The characters themselves are not rejected.
Scores below zero are representable down to -31 by lowering
\f[V]--fastq_qmin\f[R] (the offset plus the minimum must stay at 33 or
more), and \f[V]--fastq_chars\f[R] diagnoses a Solexa file correctly.
What the commands then do with a negative score differs:
\f[V]--fastq_convert\f[R] clamps it to \f[V]--fastq_qminout\f[R] (0 by
default); \f[V]--fastq_eestats\f[R] and \f[V]--fastq_eestats2\f[R] raise
it to 0; \f[V]--fastq_mergepairs\f[R] and \f[V]--fastx_uniques\f[R]
treat every score below 2 as a blind guess (p = 0.75), and
\f[V]--fastx_uniques\f[R] writes it out as Q=1; and
\f[V]--fastx_filter\f[R] discards the whole sequence, since
\f[V]--fastq_minqual\f[R] defaults to 0 and may not be negative.
.PP
The parser accepts every printable ASCII character (values 33\[en]126)
in quality strings \[em] `.' and `-' are ordinary quality symbols (Q=13
and Q=12 at offset 33).
Carriage returns before the newline are accepted; any other character
(tabulations, spaces, control characters, values 128\[en]255) causes a
fatal error; nothing is stripped or warned about.
The \f[V]--fastq_qmin\f[R]/\f[V]--fastq_qmax\f[R] score bounds are
enforced by the commands that interpret quality values, not by the
parser itself.
.SS Header annotations
.PP
vsearch reads and writes several annotations embedded in sequence
headers, following the pattern \f[V][\[at];]key=value[;]\f[R].
Annotations can appear in any order, but must be joined to the label by
`;' without any whitespace: with the default header truncation, an
annotation separated from the label by a space is part of the discarded
remainder and is silently invisible (unless \f[V]--notrunclabels\f[R] is
used).
.TP
\f[V][\[at];]size=integer[;]\f[R]
Abundance (number of occurrences of the sequence in the study).
Read by \f[V]--sizein\f[R], written by \f[V]--sizeout\f[R], removed by
\f[V]--xsize\f[R].
.TP
\f[V][\[at];]ee=float[;]\f[R]
Expected error count.
Written by \f[V]--eeout\f[R] or \f[V]--fastq_eeout\f[R], removed by
\f[V]--xee\f[R].
See \f[V]vsearch-expected_error(7)\f[R].
.TP
\f[V][\[at];]length=integer[;]\f[R]
Sequence length.
Written by \f[V]--lengthout\f[R], removed by \f[V]--xlength\f[R].
.TP
\f[V][\[at];]sample=string[;]\f[R]
Sample identifier.
Written by \f[V]--sample\f[R].
.SS Case and masking
.PP
vsearch operations are case-insensitive, except when soft masking is
active.
Lower-case letters indicate masked residues; upper-case letters indicate
unmasked residues.
DUST masking (automatic low-complexity masking applied during chimera
detection, clustering, masking, pairwise alignment, and searching)
converts low-complexity regions to lower case.
Soft masking is enabled with \f[V]--qmask soft\f[R] or
\f[V]--dbmask soft\f[R].
Masked residues are excluded from word-based comparisons but otherwise
treated as normal symbols.
.SS T and U equivalence
.PP
When comparing sequences (chimera detection, dereplication, searching,
and clustering), T and U are treated as identical regardless of case.
